      <gmd:abstract>
        <gco:CharacterString>On 10 September 2023, storm Daniel reached its peak in north-eastern Libya and caused damages and major destruction. One of the worst affected cities was Derna, at the time of event home to 90,000 people. As aftermath of the heavy rainfall the two ageing dams located in the Wadi Derna valley collapsed not being able to resist the huge volume of water. This led to a catastrophic event in which large parts of the city were destroyed and thousands of people lost their lives. One of the consequences of this event is that the city of Derna is finding itself once more exposed to floods, without mitigation systems installed protecting the inhabitants and the town. To better understand the flood risk of the area, analyses based on different scenarios are needed to retrieve insights on the possible impact of future events. The scope of the service request of EMSN177 is to support the EU environmental experts deployed within the UNDAC Team in Derna by providing them with analyses of the flood hazard and the exposure assessment of people and infrastructures based on three scenarios and mitigation measures. The impacts of the following scenarios were elaborated: floods with a (1) low probability (return period ≥ 500 years or extreme event scenarios), (2) medium probability (return period ≥ 100 years) and (3) high probability (return period of five years).</gco:CharacterString>
      </gmd:abstract>
